    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## What I did\n\nReject creating an owner when the target city already holds 50+ owners, responding 409 \u2014 implemented as small, cohesive new units mirroring the existing household-uniqueness rule, so I didn't grow any existing class:\n\n- **`EnsureCityCapacity`** (new function) \u2014 counts owners in the request's city case-insensitively and throws when the count is \u2265 50.\n- **`CityAtCapacityException`** (new checked exception) \u2014 extends `Exception` so it surfaces on the `throws` clause.\n- **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** (new handler) \u2014 responds `409 Conflict`.\n- **`...CityAtCapacityException.yml`** (new escalation mapping) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** (edited) \u2014 inserted the `ensureCityCapacity` step between `ensureUniqueHousehold` and `build`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\nStructural impact was kept minimal: the only edited existing function is the YAML pipeline wiring (a 4-line insertion); all logic lives in brand-new classes with essentially zero surrounding complexity (`WMC_other \u2248 0`, low CC), rather than being bolted onto an existing method or class.",
